  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Adnan Siraj Ahamad Shaikh vs State Of Maharashtra on 20 February, 2026 2026:Bhc-As:8775 Ajit Pathrikar In The High Court Of Judicature At Bombay Criminal Appellate Jurisdiction Bail Application No. 705 Of 2026 Adnan Siraj Ahamad Shaikh ...App Versus State Of Maharashtra ...",
    "facts": "By this Application, the Applicant seeks his enlargement on bail in connection with C.R.No. 20 of 2026 dated 15 th January, 2026 registered with the Mira Road Police Station, District Mira Bhayandar, Vasai Virar, for the offences punishable under Sections 123, 223, 272, 275 and 3(5) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023 ('BNS') and Sections 26(2), 26(4) and 30(2)(a) of the Food Safety and Standard Act, Page 1 of 5 th 20 February 2026 Adnan Siraj Ahamad Shaikh vs State Of Maharashtra on 20 February, 2026 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/187203329/ 1 ::: Uploaded on - 20/02/2026 ::: Downloaded 2006 and Section 7 of the Prohibition of Electronic Cigarettes Act, 2019. 2. The Applicant made an application seeking bail before the Additional Sessions Judge, Thane, however, by order dated 29th January, 2026, the said application was rejected. Hence, the Applicant has filed the present Bail Application for the relief as prayed.",
    "arguments": "3. Mr. Abhinav Utekar, learned counsel for the Applicant, pointed out that the issue is pending before the Supreme Court. He submits that the Applicant has suffered incarceration for the past one month. Moreover, this Court has already held that mere possession, transportation, storage or sale of Pan Masala packets would not attract Section 328 of the IPC (Section 123 of the BNS) and the said issue is now pending before the Supreme Court. 4. Mr. Mayur Sonavane, learned APP representing the State, states that the charge-sheet is not yet filed and the Applicant has antecedent in similar offences.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "3. Mr. Abhinav Utekar, learned counsel for the Applicant, pointed out that the issue is pending before the Supreme Court. He submits that the Applicant has suffered incarceration for the past one month. Moreover, this Court has already held that mere possession, transportation, storage or sale of Pan Masala packets would not attract Section 328 of the IPC (Section 123 of the BNS) and the said issue is now pending before the Supreme Court. 4. Mr. Mayur Sonavane, learned APP representing the State, states that the charge-sheet is not yet filed and the Applicant has antecedent in similar offences.",
